  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The model's demonstration relies on hypothetical 5% mode shift scenarios rather than observed policy interventions.",
      "what": "The open-source Integrated Transport and Health Impact Modelling Tool for Global Cities (ITHIM-Global) was developed to simulate how shifts in transport modes affect carbon dioxide emissions, road traffic fatalities, air pollution exposure, and physical activity. Using a quasi-microsimulation approach, the model estimates age- and sex-specific mortality and years of life lost using non-linear dose-response functions. Its application was demonstrated in Bogotá, Colombia, by modeling 5% trip shifts to buses, cars, or cycling, showing that public and active transit shifts yield health benefits primarily through increased physical activity.",
      "why": "It provides a flexible, open-source health impact assessment tool specifically designed for low- and middle-income countries, where rapid urbanization demands accessible tools to quantify the health and environmental trade-offs of transport planning decisions."
    }
